[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday September 30th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  you might remember that last year we  brought you a special three-part series  called The Tick Tock Spyro  a Wall Street Journal investigation  found that tick tock's algorithm was  flooding some teens for you pages with  videos encouraging weight loss and  disordered eating we heard from three of  them  I saw a lot of videos of people saying  what I eat in a day it made me start  thinking maybe I should start purging to  get rid of the weight eventually there  were like some self-harm and suicide a  lot of ideation I would just swipe and  that's it my feet was literally like a  mostly consisting of that the series was  part of an even bigger wsj investigation  that showed tick tock's recommendation  algorithm would funnel users into seeing  videos on a narrow range of topics last  December Tick Tock which is owned by  Chinese parent bite dance said that it  was adjusting the way its algorithm  makes recommendations to avoid overly  focusing on one type of content and two  months later the company expanded an  existing ban on content related to  eating disorders but nine months on from  the wsj investigation there's still  plenty of that harmful content on tick  tock here to discuss why is our family  and Tech columnist Julie jargon hi Julie  thanks for joining us hi thank you for  having me all right you spoke with users  who say they are still seeing eating  disorder content when they're on Tick  Tock what kinds of things did they  describe were coming up on their for you  page  yeah it seems to have changed from being  really blatant videos that are promoting  disordered eating and Extreme Weight  Loss it's become a bit more subtle and  the difficult thing about that according  to mental health experts is that it's  harder for teens to discern what is  healthy diet related videos from  unhealthy and so some examples of that  include a topic of videos that are kind  of broadly Under the Umbrella of what I  eat in a day and oftentimes these videos  will have you know the people in the  video showing what they eat throughout a  day and how much calories they consume  by the end of the day oftentimes the  calorie count is far less than what the  FDA recommends people consume in a day  and so for people who may really need to  lose weight that could potentially be  helpful but to a lot of people that can  be harmful if they're trying to emulate  sort of restricted food intake  throughout the course of a day another  category of content on Tick Tock which  Tick Tock says it allows on its platform  our eating disorder recovery videos and  for some people it can be really helpful  to see the journey that someone has gone  through in their recovery from an eating  disorder but for a lot of teens that  I've spoken to those kind of videos can  end up being kind of harmful and end up  fueling some competition in them various  eating disorder experts say that Eating  Disorders are a very competitive type of  disorder to have and so in some of these  videos people will show photos of  themselves before and after so showing  photos when they were at their thinnest  versus where they are in their recovery  now and so some teens I've spoke to said  that that makes them feel like maybe  they didn't lose as much weight does  that other person did also sometimes  sows doubts in them about whether they  themselves were ever as sick as the  person they're seeing in that video so  Tick Tock says it allows recovery videos  but it has put in place restrictions to  block other harmful content and yet  these videos make it onto the platform  is this just about videos falling into a  gray area when it comes to being harmful  or are there other ways that users are  getting around this it's a bit of both  you know certainly there are some videos  that are more nuanced and might not be  considered a blatant violation of tick  tock's Community guidelines they might  not be pro-eating disorder per se and  could can in fact be helpful and  inspiring to some people who see them  and potentially triggering to others who  see them but then there are ways that  people have found to kind of get around  some of tick tock's filters so for  example  certain obvious eating disorder related  terms have been filtered out on Tick  Tock so if you're searching the word  anorexic in Tick Tock search engine you  won't find videos that contain that  hashtag instead you'll be directed to  messages from the national Eating  Disorders Association but until recently  people could simply do a Google search  and search the hashtag anorexic Tick  Tock and go right to those videos that  contain that hashtag and it might be  more in the realm of pro-eating disorder  content I brought that to tick tock's  attention and they have fixed that but  there are a lot of other workarounds  including misspellings of eating  disorder related terms transposing  letters including numbers different ways  to get around the hashtag filters so has  Tick Tock said anything about the  content still being on its platform what  they said to me was that caring for a  diverse Global Community is a  responsibility they take seriously and  that they're mindful that triggering  content is varied and unique to each  individual  how do mental health and eating disorder  experts say they're dealing with  patients who are you know using social  media and finding themselves in this  position because it just doesn't seem  like it's going away that's right a  mental health experts say that there  probably will never be a completely safe  space online for people who are  struggling with really any kind of  mental health issue and eating disorders  in particular there's just a pervasive  diet culture online to begin with and  offline as well what some mental health  experts have told me is that it's you  know unfortunately Falls to people who  are using social media to try to run  away from this content and to try to  block it themselves you know one mental  health expert said she had a patient who  basically had to retrain her Tick-Tock  algorithm by not only unfollowing diet  related accounts but then following body  positive content creators and liking  their videos to indicate to the  algorithm that she wanted to see that  type of content but again it it's not  perfect and it's also a big ask to  expect someone who's struggling with  something to make a conscious decision  to get away from it alright that was our  family and Tech columnist Julie jargon  Julie thanks so much for joining us  thank you for having me  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our producer is Julie Chang we  had editorial assistance this week from  Ariana oscaru our supervising producer  is Chris Tinsley our executive producer  is Katara Yocum and I'm your host Zoe  Thomas thanks for listening and have a  great weekend  foreign

